<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Orthodoxy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Byzantine Christianity, Eastern Orthodox Church</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eastern Orthodoxy, also called Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, is one of the three main branches of Chalcedonian Christianity, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and certain forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. It spread from the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> within Byzantine Greek culture and is organised into 17 mainstream autocephalous churches that choose their own primates, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Ecumenical Patriarchate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. It rejects the Filioque clause added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> and holds to the doctrines of the seven ecumenical councils. Roughly half of its adherents live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and it is the majority faith across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p></div>
